Below is a step-by-step recipe you can copy-paste into a MapLibre GL JS app (or into a style JSON you edit by hand).  
It creates a **raster-DEM source**, points it at a tile service that returns 16-bit elevation tiles, and then adds a **hillshade layer** that visualises the terrain.

---

## 1. Make sure you have a DEM tile service

MapLibre expects the DEM tiles to be in **Mapbox-compatible raster-DEM format** (16-bit unsigned integer, elevation in meters).  
Typical providers:

| Provider | URL template | Notes |
|----------|--------------|-------|
| **MapLibre demo tiles** | `https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Small world-wide DEM (≈ 30 m) – great for testing |
| **OpenMapTiles Terrain** | `https://tiles.openmaptiles.org/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Requires an API key for production |
| **Your own MBTiles/GeoServer** | `https://myserver.com/tiles/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Must serve PNG/WEBP with `Content-Type: image/png` (or `image/webp`) and CORS headers |

> **Tip:** The DEM tiles must be *single-band* (the elevation value is stored in the red channel). If you have a multi-band GeoTIFF you’ll need to convert it (e.g. with `gdal_translate -b 1 -of PNG`).

---

## 2. Add the raster-DEM source to the map

```js
// Assuming you already have a MapLibre map instance called `map`
map.addSource('my-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // The URL template from step 1
  url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // Optional but recommended
  tileSize: 256,          // 256 px tiles (most services) – set 512 if your source uses that size
  maxzoom: 14,           // highest zoom level your DEM tiles support
  // If your DEM tiles are encoded as 16-bit signed integers, set `encoding: "mapbox"` (default)
  // encoding: "mapbox", // for unsigned 16-bit (default)
});
```

*If you are loading the source **before** the map is ready, wrap the call in `map.on('load', …)`.*

---

## 3. Add the hillshade layer

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'my-dem',
  // The raster-DEM source can be used directly; no need for a separate terrain source.
  paint: {
    // Optional – tweak the visual look
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315, // degrees clockwise from north (default 315)
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,           // 0 = flat, 1 = real elevation, >1 exaggerates
    'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000000',
    'hillshade-shadow-color-transition': {duration: 0},
    'hillshade-highlight-color': '#ffffff',
    'hillshade-accent-color': '#777777',
    // You can also control opacity:
    // 'hillshade-opacity': 0.7,
  },
});
```

### What the paint properties do

| Property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------|---------------|--------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | 0-360° | Sun direction; 315° (north-west) is the Mapbox default. |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | 0-5+ | Multiplies the elevation. Use >1 for dramatic relief, <1 for subtle. |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` / `hillshade-highlight-color` / `hillshade-accent-color` | CSS color | Colors for the three shading tones (shadow, highlight, mid-tone). |
| `hillshade-opacity` | 0-1 | Overall opacity of the hillshade layer. |

---

## 4. (Optional) Add a **terrain** source for 3-D extrusion

If you also want to **extrude 3-D buildings, drape raster layers, or enable the globe’s terrain**, add a separate `terrain` source that points at the same DEM:

```js
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'my-dem',   // reuse the raster-dem source
  exaggeration: 1.5   // same concept as hillshade-exaggeration, but for 3-D terrain
});
```

> **Note:** The hillshade layer works *without* `setTerrain()`. Adding terrain is only needed for 3-D visualisation.

---

## 5. Full minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hillshade from raster-DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base map (any vector source you like)
        'osm': {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/gl/voyager-gl-style/style.json'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'background',
          type: 'background',
          paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e0e0' }
        },
        // Add the vector base layers you need here …
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    // CORS for the demo DEM tiles is already allowed
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source
    map.addSource('my-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain-tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 14
    });

    // 2️⃣ Hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'my-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.7,
        'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000000',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': '#ffffff',
        'hillshade-accent-color': '#777777'
      }
    });

    // (Optional) 3️⃣ 3-D terrain
    // map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.2 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser – you should see a faint gray-white relief over the base map. Zoom in/out; the hillshade updates automatically.

---

## 6. Common pitfalls & troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No hillshade appears (just the base map)** | DEM source not loading (404, CORS, wrong URL) | Open the network tab, verify the DEM tiles are fetched successfully. Add `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on the tile server or proxy. |
| **Hillshade looks flat (no relief)** | `hillshade-exaggeration` is `0` or DEM elevation values are all zero | Check the DEM tiles with a viewer (e.g. QGIS) to confirm they contain elevation. Increase `hillshade-exaggeration`. |
| **Colors look wrong / banding** | Using a PNG that stores elevation in the *green* channel instead of red, or using a signed-int encoding | Ensure the DEM tiles follow Mapbox’s raster-DEM spec (elevation in the red channel, unsigned 16-bit). If you have a custom format, convert with `gdal_translate -b 1 -scale 0 65535 -ot UInt16`. |
| **Hillshade flickers when panning** | `tileSize` mismatch (source uses 512 px tiles but you set 256) | Set `tileSize` to match the source (most services use 256). |
| **CORS error on local development** | Browser blocks cross-origin tile fetches | Run a local dev server (`npm start`, `python -m http.server`, etc.) and make sure the DEM server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` for your origin. |
| **You want the hillshade to appear *above* other layers** | Layer order | Add the hillshade layer **after** the layers you want it to overlay, or use `map.moveLayer('hillshade', 'some-other-layer-id')` to reposition it. |

---

## 7. Quick checklist

- [ ] DEM tiles are reachable (`{z}/{x}/{y}.png` or `.webp`) and CORS-enabled.  
- [ ] Source type is **`raster-dem`** (not `raster`).  
- [ ] `tileSize` matches the service (256 px is default).  
- [ ] Hillshade layer `type: 'hillshade'` references the DEM source.  
- [ ] Paint properties set to desired visual style (exaggeration, illumination direction, colors).  
- [ ] (Optional) `map.setTerrain({source: 'my-dem'})` if you need 3-D terrain.  

That’s it! You now have a working hillshade layer driven by a raster-DEM source in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping.
